# Line-ending policy for artifacts consumed inside Linux containers.
#
# The repository is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, so without a
# pin git writes CRLF into the working tree -- and the working tree *is* the
# Docker build context. A CRLF shell script fails to exec in the image
# (`#!/bin/sh`: no such file or directory), and CRLF line continuations break
# multi-line RUN instructions. Every one of these blobs is already LF in the
# index, so this pin renormalises nothing; it only stops the checkout from
# corrupting them.
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf
entrypoint.sh text eol=lf

# Debian packaging (wave L4). Same hazard, sharper: dpkg-buildpackage execs
# debian/rules directly and runs the maintainer scripts, so a CRLF checkout
# yields `#!/bin/sh^M: not found` and the build or the install fails. The
# maintainer scripts carry no .sh suffix, so the *.sh pin above does not reach
# them. Blobs are already LF; this only stops a Windows checkout corrupting them.
debian/** text eol=lf

# RETIRED (§10.5b, ORCH_PROTOCOL.md, 2026-09-19): this section used to pin
# `merge=ours` on docs/API.md, docs/_generated/**, docs/vertical_engines/
# ENGINE_STATUS.md, and openapi.json (K-H7, 2026-09-18) to lower the cost of
# the rebase conflicts those generated files caused on every PR. K-H9
# (2026-09-19) found the driver is worse than a stopgap: "ours" flips meaning
# between `merge` and `rebase` (under rebase it means the branch being
# rebased ONTO), so on a branch's SECOND rebase it silently replays a stale
# copy over an already-correct regenerated one, with zero conflict markers --
# the exact hazard this driver existed to hide the SIGNAL of, not the
# problem. See ORCH_PROTOCOL.md §10.5a for the incident.
#
# §10.5b removes the actual cause instead of dulling its edge: PRs no longer
# carry these files at all (nor docs/_generated/host_parity.md, same class,
# same glob, added to the same regeneration job for completeness). A
# .github/workflows/regen-generated-docs.yml job regenerates and commits them
# on `main` after every merge, only when the diff is non-empty. With no
# generated file in a PR's diff, there is nothing left for a rebase to
# corrupt -- this driver's failure mode cannot fire on a file that was never
# there to conflict on. The old `git config merge.ours.driver true` one-time
# setup is no longer needed for these paths; nothing else in this file used
# it.
